Resolve out of a surface held at sample resolution: a 1/k downscale on the GPU (Metal, Vulkan)

- Since the toolkit keeps an antialiased target at sample resolution, a title's resolve out of it (this game: six 2:1
  NV3089 tiles, INTERPOLATOR_FOH, source origin at the texel corner, +1 texel offset) is a downscale of the host
  surface, not the 1:1 copy rsx_draw_engine_resolve_read assumed for a surface drawn at display size. With no match
  it went through the guest-memory copy, which reads the whole 2560x1440 surface back every frame.
- rsx_draw_backend gains an optional color_target_resolve(dst, dx, dy, src, sx, sy, w, h, k): each destination pixel
  is the linear sample at the centre of its k x k block of the source, edge repeated. The engine uses it to fill a
  display-sized target it keeps for the guest image, with the machinery the 1:1 GPU resolve already has (pending
  rectangles, store to guest memory on demand, the target sampled as the final pass's texture).
- Metal: a stream record that runs one render pass with a linear sampler. Vulkan: linear blits, one for the blocks
  inside the source and one per pixel whose block crosses its edge.
- NV3089 passes whether the blit interpolates from a corner origin; anything else keeps the guest-memory copy.
- RSX_ENGINE_GPU_RESOLVE=0 turns it off, as for the 1:1 resolve.

